PULSE-SAFE AMR: A Verifiable Safety Framework for Autonomous Mobile Robots in Mixed Industrial Environments

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This dissertation presents a complete theoretical framework for PULSE-SAFE AMR, an independent safety monitor that enforces a verifiable safety envelope for autonomous mobile robots operating in mixed industrial environments. The central contribution is a mathematical formalization of the safety condition grounded in hybrid dynamical system theory, differential dynamic logic, barrier certificate verification, and conservative physical modeling of braking, adhesion, and stability under combined slip conditions. We establish necessary and sufficient conditions for passive safety, prove the safety invariant via mechanized theorem proving in KeYmaera X, and extend the framework to turning trajectories via algebraic ghost-variable encoding that preserves polynomial structure for quantifier elimination. We introduce a Time-to-Danger metric that complements passive safety with graduated response while preserving the formal guarantee as a hard constraint, and we develop a three-layer barrier certificate architecture combining analytic proofs, data-driven neural certificates verified via branch-and-bound, and conservative fallback. The friction circle constraint is derived from first principles, yielding a coupled braking-deceleration limit that replaces the constant deceleration parameter with a state-dependent function a_{\max}(v_r, \omega) = \sqrt{(\mu g)^2 - (v_r\omega)^2}. Real-time friction estimation is formalized via recursive least squares, Burckhardt slip-slope analysis, and active micro-pulse probing, with conservative statistical bounding for safety integrity. A five-function Safety Requirements Specification is defined with traceable Performance Level targets and full-chain PFHd allocation across sensor, logic, and actuator subsystems. The architecture enforces deterministic dual-channel processing with hardware-enforced safe states and formalized channel independence. The framework is mapped to ISO 3691-4:2024 operating area classifications and structured as a Goal Structuring Notation safety case with runtime evidence logging. This document constitutes a theoretical specification, not a certified design.

