Chapter 2: Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Hybrid Dynamical Systems

Definition 2.1 (Hybrid Dynamical System). A hybrid dynamical system is a tuple \mathcal{H} = (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{U}, \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{G}, \mathcal{R}) where:

· \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n is the continuous state space,
· \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m is the control input space,
· \mathcal{F}: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^n is the continuous vector field,
· \mathcal{G} \subseteq \mathcal{X} is the guard set (discrete transition enabling conditions),
· \mathcal{R}: \mathcal{X} \rightarrow \mathcal{X} is the reset map.

The system evolves according to \dot{\xi} = \mathcal{F}(\xi, u) during continuous flow, and \xi^+ = \mathcal{R}(\xi) upon discrete transition when \xi \in \mathcal{G}.

2.2 Differential Dynamic Logic

Definition 2.2 (Hybrid Program). The set of hybrid programs is generated by the grammar:

\alpha ::= x := e \mid x := * \mid ?Q \mid \alpha_1; \alpha_2 \mid \alpha_1 \cup \alpha_2 \mid \alpha^* \mid \{\dot{x} = f(x) \ \&\ Q\}

where x := e is a discrete assignment, x := * is non-deterministic assignment, ?Q is a test (assertion), ; is sequential composition, \cup is non-deterministic choice, ^* is non-deterministic repetition, and \{\dot{x} = f(x) \ \&\ Q\} is continuous evolution along the differential equation \dot{x} = f(x) restricted to the domain Q.

Definition 2.3 (dL Formula). The set of dL formulas is generated by:

\phi ::= P \mid \neg\phi \mid \phi_1 \land \phi_2 \mid \phi_1 \lor \phi_2 \mid \phi_1 \rightarrow \phi_2 \mid \forall x \, \phi \mid \exists x \, \phi \mid [\alpha]\phi \mid \langle\alpha\rangle\phi

where P is a first-order real arithmetic predicate, [\alpha]\phi is the box modality (necessarily after all executions of \alpha, \phi holds), and \langle\alpha\rangle\phi is the diamond modality (there exists an execution of \alpha after which \phi holds).

Definition 2.4 (Semantics). The satisfaction relation \omega \models \phi for dL formula \phi in state \omega is defined recursively. The box modality has semantics:

\omega \models [\alpha]\phi \iff \text{for all } \nu \text{ reachable from } \omega \text{ via } \alpha, \ \nu \models \phi

2.3 System Model

Definition 2.5 (AMR State). The AMR state is \mathbf{x} = (\mathbf{p}_r, v_r, \theta_r) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathbb{R} \times \mathcal{S}^1, where \mathbf{p}_r = (p_{rx}, p_{ry}) is position, v_r is scalar speed, and \theta_r is heading.

Definition 2.6 (Obstacle State). The obstacle state is \mathbf{x}_o = (\mathbf{p}_o, \mathbf{v}_o) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathbb{R}^2, where \mathbf{p}_o is position and \mathbf{v}_o = (v_{ox}, v_{oy}) is velocity.

Definition 2.7 (Full State). The full state vector is:

\xi = (p_{rx}, p_{ry}, v_r, \theta_r, p_{ox}, p_{oy}, v_{ox}, v_{oy}) \in \mathbb{R}^8

Definition 2.8 (Dynamics). The AMR dynamics are governed by:

\dot{\mathbf{p}}_r = v_r \cdot \mathbf{d}_r, \quad \dot{v}_r = a, \quad \dot{\theta}_r = \omega

where \mathbf{d}_r = (\cos\theta_r, \sin\theta_r), a \in [-b, a_{\max}] is longitudinal acceleration, and \omega \in [-\omega_{\max}, \omega_{\max}] is angular velocity. The obstacle evolves as \dot{\mathbf{p}}_o = \mathbf{v}_o with \|\mathbf{v}_o\| \leq V.

