Chapter 4: Barrier Certificate Theory

4.1 Analytic Barrier Certificate

Definition 4.1 (Barrier Certificate). A function B: \mathbb{R}^n \rightarrow \mathbb{R} is a barrier certificate for the safe set \mathcal{X}_s if:

1. B(\xi) \leq 0 for all \xi \in \mathcal{X}_0 (initial set),
2. B(\xi) > 0 for all \xi \in \mathcal{X}_u (unsafe set),
3. \dot{B}(\xi) \leq 0 for all \xi on the boundary B(\xi) = 0.

The certified safe set is \mathcal{X}_s = \{\xi : B(\xi) \leq 0\}.

Definition 4.2 (Analytic Barrier Certificate). The analytic barrier certificate for PULSE-SAFE is:

B_{\text{ana}}(\xi) = \frac{v_r^2}{2b} + V \frac{v_r}{b} - \|\mathbf{p}_r - \mathbf{p}_o\|

Lemma 4.1. The condition B_{\text{ana}}(\xi) \leq 0 is exactly the passive safety condition \phi_{\text{ps}}.

Proof. B_{\text{ana}}(\xi) \leq 0 rearranges to \|\mathbf{p}_r - \mathbf{p}_o\| \geq v_r^2/(2b) + V v_r/b, which is \phi_{\text{ps}} (with non-strict inequality; the strict version is \phi_{\text{ps}}). \square

Theorem 4.1 (Barrier Certificate Correctness). The analytic barrier certificate B_{\text{ana}} satisfies the three barrier certificate conditions under braking control.

Proof.

1. Initial set: By Theorem 3.2, the initial condition \psi_{\text{ps}} implies \phi_{\text{ps}}, hence B_{\text{ana}}(\xi) \leq 0.
2. Unsafe set: The unsafe set \mathcal{X}_u is \{\xi : \|\mathbf{p}_r - \mathbf{p}_o\| \leq 0\}, i.e., collision. For such \xi, B_{\text{ana}}(\xi) = v_r^2/(2b) + V v_r/b - 0 > 0 for v_r > 0.
3. Boundary condition: On the boundary B_{\text{ana}}(\xi) = 0, compute the Lie derivative:

\dot{B}_{\text{ana}} = \frac{v_r}{b} \dot{v}_r + \frac{V}{b} \dot{v}_r - \frac{d}{dt}\|\mathbf{p}_r - \mathbf{p}_o\|

Under braking \dot{v}_r = -b:

\dot{B}_{\text{ana}} = -\frac{v_r}{b} b - \frac{V}{b} b - \frac{d}{dt}\|\mathbf{p}_r - \mathbf{p}_o\| = -(v_r + V) - \frac{d}{dt}\|\mathbf{p}_r - \mathbf{p}_o\|

Since \frac{d}{dt}\|\mathbf{p}_r - \mathbf{p}_o\| \geq -(v_r + V):

\dot{B}_{\text{ana}} \leq -(v_r + V) + (v_r + V) = 0

\square

4.2 Neural Barrier Certificate

Definition 4.3 (Neural Barrier Certificate). A neural barrier certificate B_{\text{NN}}: \mathbb{R}^n \rightarrow \mathbb{R} is a ReLU neural network satisfying the three barrier certificate conditions.

Definition 4.4 (ReLU Network). A ReLU network is a function f: \mathbb{R}^n \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^m of the form:

f(x) = W_L \sigma(W_{L-1} \sigma(\cdots \sigma(W_1 x + b_1) \cdots) + b_{L-1}) + b_L

where \sigma(z) = \max(0, z) is the ReLU activation, W_i are weight matrices, and b_i are bias vectors.

Theorem 4.2 (Necessary and Sufficient Condition for ReLU Barrier Certificate). A ReLU network B_{\text{NN}} is a valid barrier certificate if and only if the following optimization problem has a non-positive optimum:

\min_{\xi \in \mathcal{X}} \max\left( B_{\text{NN}}(\xi), \ -B_{\text{NN}}(\xi) + \epsilon, \ -\dot{B}_{\text{NN}}(\xi) \right) \leq 0

where \mathcal{X} = \mathcal{X}_0 \cup \mathcal{X}_u \cup \partial\mathcal{X}_s is the union of the initial set, unsafe set, and boundary.

Proof. The condition encodes the three barrier certificate requirements: (i) B_{\text{NN}}(\xi) \leq 0 on \mathcal{X}_0, (ii) B_{\text{NN}}(\xi) > 0 on \mathcal{X}_u (encoded as -B_{\text{NN}}(\xi) + \epsilon \leq 0 for margin \epsilon > 0), and (iii) \dot{B}_{\text{NN}}(\xi) \leq 0 on \partial\mathcal{X}_s. The max formulation ensures all three conditions are satisfied simultaneously. \square

Remark 4.1. The verification of ReLU barrier certificates is encoded as an SMT or optimization problem, enabling both verification and falsification. The piecewise-linear structure of ReLU networks allows exact verification via branch-and-bound with convex relaxations.

4.3 Data-Driven Barrier Certificate via Matrix Zonotopes

Definition 4.5 (Matrix Zonotope). A matrix zonotope is a set:

\mathcal{MZ} = \left\{ M_0 + \sum_{i=1}^{N} \alpha_i M_i : \alpha_i \in [-1, 1] \right\}

where M_0 is the center matrix and M_i are the generator matrices.

Definition 4.6 (Data-Consistent Model Set). Given noisy observations \{(\xi_k, \xi_{k+1})\}_{k=1}^{N}, the data-consistent model set is:

\Sigma = \left\{ (A, B) : \xi_{k+1} = A \xi_k + B u_k + w_k, \ \|w_k\| \leq \epsilon \right\}

Theorem 4.3 (Data-Driven Barrier Certificate). If there exists a barrier certificate B satisfying the three conditions for all models in the data-consistent model set \Sigma, then B is a valid barrier certificate for the true system.

Proof. By construction, the true system model is contained in \Sigma. If the barrier conditions hold for all models in \Sigma, they hold in particular for the true model. \square

Architecture 4.1 (Three-Layer Certificate System).

1. Formal core: KeYmaera X proof of Theorem 3.2 for the idealized braking model, providing the normative proof certificate.
2. Runtime certificate: Neural barrier certificate B_{\text{NN}} trained on hardware data (braking behavior, actuator delays, tire slip, surface variations). The runtime monitor verifies B_{\text{NN}}(\xi) \leq 0.
3. Conservative fallback: If B_{\text{NN}} cannot be evaluated due to sensor fault or numerical uncertainty, revert to B_{\text{ana}}(\xi) \leq 0.

Theorem 4.4 (Soundness of Three-Layer Architecture). The three-layer architecture is sound: if any layer certifies safety, the system is safe.

Proof. The formal core certifies safety by Theorem 3.2. The runtime certificate certifies safety by Theorem 4.2 (if verified offline). The conservative fallback certifies safety by Theorem 4.1. Since all three layers certify the same safety property, satisfaction of any one is sufficient. \square

