Chapter 5: Real-Time Friction Estimation

5.1 Problem Formulation

Problem 5.1. The friction coefficient \mu is unknown and time-varying. Assuming a static value (e.g., \mu = 0.7 for clean concrete) is a safety hazard: if the AMR encounters a polished epoxy floor, wet patch, or spilled debris, \mu can drop to 0.2 or lower, causing the robot to skid past its calculated stopping distance.

Definition 5.1 (Friction Estimation Problem). Given measurements of commanded force F_{\text{cmd}}, measured acceleration a_{\text{imu}}, wheel speed \omega_w, and vehicle speed v_r, estimate \mu(t) in real time with bounded error.

5.2 Inertial-Odometric Mismatch via Recursive Least Squares

Definition 5.2 (Regressor Formulation). The friction estimation problem can be formulated as a linear regression:

y(t) = \phi(t)^T \theta + \epsilon(t)

where y(t) = a_{\text{imu}}(t) is the measured acceleration, \phi(t) = g is the regressor, \theta = \mu is the parameter to estimate, and \epsilon(t) is noise.

Definition 5.3 (Recursive Least Squares). The RLS update with forgetting factor \lambda \in (0.95, 0.99) is:

\hat{\theta}(t) = \hat{\theta}(t-1) + K(t) \left( y(t) - \phi(t)^T \hat{\theta}(t-1) \right)

K(t) = \frac{P(t-1)\phi(t)}{\lambda + \phi(t)^T P(t-1) \phi(t)}

P(t) = \frac{1}{\lambda} \left( P(t-1) - \frac{P(t-1)\phi(t)\phi(t)^T P(t-1)}{\lambda + \phi(t)^T P(t-1)\phi(t)} \right)

Theorem 5.1 (RLS Convergence). Under persistent excitation (\sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \phi(t)\phi(t)^T = \infty) and bounded noise, the RLS estimate converges: \hat{\theta}(t) \rightarrow \theta as t \rightarrow \infty.

Proof. This is the standard convergence result for RLS with forgetting factor. The forgetting factor ensures tracking of time-varying parameters, while persistent excitation ensures parameter identifiability. \square

5.3 Slip-Slope Analysis via Burckhardt Model

Definition 5.4 (Slip Ratio). The slip ratio is:

s = \frac{\omega_w r - v_r}{\max(\omega_w r, v_r)}

where \omega_w is wheel angular velocity and r is wheel radius.

Definition 5.5 (Burckhardt Tire Model). The friction coefficient as a function of slip is:

\mu(s) = c_1 \left( 1 - e^{-c_2 s} \right) - c_3 s

where c_1, c_2, c_3 are surface-dependent coefficients.

Theorem 5.2 (Slip-Slope Friction Estimation). In the micro-slip region |s| < 0.05, the slope of the friction curve \partial \mu / \partial s is proportional to the peak friction coefficient:

\left. \frac{\partial \mu}{\partial s} \right|_{s=0} = c_1 c_2

Proof. Differentiate the Burckhardt model:

\frac{\partial \mu}{\partial s} = c_1 c_2 e^{-c_2 s} - c_3

At s = 0:

\left. \frac{\partial \mu}{\partial s} \right|_{s=0} = c_1 c_2 - c_3

For typical surfaces, c_3 \ll c_1 c_2, so the initial slope is approximately c_1 c_2, which is proportional to the peak friction. \square

5.4 Active Probing via Micro-Pulse Injection

Definition 5.6 (Micro-Pulse Injection). During constant-velocity cruising, inject a high-frequency micro-torque pulse \Delta \tau of duration < 20 ms into the drive motors.

Definition 5.7 (Contact Stiffness). The contact stiffness is estimated as:

k = \frac{\Delta a}{\Delta \tau}

where \Delta a is the measured acceleration response.

Theorem 5.3 (Stiffness-Friction Correlation). The contact stiffness k correlates monotonically with the friction coefficient \mu:

\frac{\partial k}{\partial \mu} > 0

Proof. This follows from the physics of tire-road contact: higher friction surfaces (rubber, concrete) exhibit higher tangential stiffness than low-friction surfaces (ice, oil). The monotonic relationship is established empirically in tire mechanics literature. \square

5.5 Conservative Bounding for Safety

Definition 5.8 (Safe Friction Bound). The safety monitor uses a conservative statistical lower bound:

\mu_{\text{safe}}(t) = \hat{\mu}(t) - k \cdot \sigma_{\mu}(t)

where \sigma_{\mu}(t) is the estimation variance and k \in [2.5, 3.0] is a safety factor corresponding to a 99.7% confidence interval.

Theorem 5.4 (Safety Guarantee Under Friction Uncertainty). If the true friction \mu_{\text{true}} satisfies \mu_{\text{true}} \geq \mu_{\text{safe}}, then the dynamic passive safety condition \phi_{\text{ps, dynamic}} with \mu = \mu_{\text{safe}} is conservative.

Proof. Since a_{\max}(v_r, \omega; \mu) = \sqrt{(\mu g)^2 - (v_r \omega)^2} is monotonically increasing in \mu, using \mu_{\text{safe}} \leq \mu_{\text{true}} yields a_{\max}(\mu_{\text{safe}}) \leq a_{\max}(\mu_{\text{true}}), hence the required separation is larger, and the condition is more conservative. \square

