Chapter 6: System Architecture

6.1 Independent Safety Layer

PULSE-SAFE is implemented as a separate safety layer with its own power supply and processing unit. It does not share the navigation controller's CPU or power bus.

Architecture 6.1 (Dual-Channel Architecture).

· Channel A: Navigation controller (path planning, MPC).
· Channel B: Independent safety monitor (TTD computation, limit verification, command authorization).
· Cross-comparison: Continuous diagnostic comparison of both channels.
· SF-5 monitor: Independent verification of channel independence.

6.2 Channel Independence

Definition 6.1 (Channel Independence). Channels A and B are independent if and only if:

1. Timing independence: \text{WCET}(B) \leq T_{\text{cycle}} regardless of Channel A's execution state.
2. Power independence: Separate power rails with monitored voltage, V_A \perp V_B.
3. Data independence: Separate sensor data paths.
4. Clock independence: Independent watchdog timers, T_A \perp T_B.

Theorem 6.1 (Necessity of Independence for Category 3). Category 3 architecture requires that a single fault cannot lead to loss of the safety function. If channels are not independent, a single fault in shared resources can cause common-cause failure, violating Category 3.

Proof. By ISO 13849-1, Category 3 requires that "a single fault cannot lead to the loss of the safety function". If channels share a resource (power, clock, data), a fault in that resource affects both channels simultaneously, constituting a common-cause failure that violates the requirement. \square

6.3 Measurement Chain

· Safety LiDAR: Type 3 per IEC 61496-1/-3, up to SIL 2/PL d, angular resolution ≤ 0.5° for detection of 70 mm objects at 4 m range. Documented response times: 60–120 ms.
· Radar or ultrasonic: Complementary detection for adverse conditions.
· Inertial measurement unit: Angular rate and acceleration.
· Encoders: Wheel odometry.
· Safety bumper: Last-resort contact detection.

6.4 Actuation and Safe State

· Safe Torque Off (STO): Removes motor torque via redundant channel switching of power semiconductors. Documented response time: 2–25 ms.
· Safe Brake Control (SBC): Controls the holding brake safely per IEC 61800-5-2.
· SS1 (Safe Stop 1): Brakes the motor then initiates STO after a delay.
· Redundant contactors: Physical disconnection of actuator power.

6.5 Fail-Safe Behaviour

Any of the following conditions triggers a safe stop within a defined time:

· Divergence between Channel A and Channel B,
· Loss of a safety sensor,
· Timeout in the safety monitor,
· Internal diagnostic failure,
· Violation of SF-5 independence conditions.

The safe stop is executed via STO and mechanical braking, with the stopping distance validated on hardware.

6.6 FPGA-Based Deterministic Processing

Architecture 6.2 (Latency-Optimized Safety Path). To achieve the reaction time budget required for 1.0 m/s operation, the safety path uses:

1. Direct LiDAR-to-FPGA routing: Ethernet frames bypass the ROS/middleware stack and are processed deterministically at 200 Hz.
2. Bare-metal safety MCU: Channel B evaluation in 5 ms.
3. Semiconductor-based STO: IGBT/MOSFET hardware gating with < 5 ms response.

Documented latency reduction: 500× speedup over software implementation.

