Appendix B: Ghost-Variable Encoding for Turning Trajectories

B.1 Problem Statement

Standard quantifier elimination procedures operate strictly on polynomial real arithmetic. Trigonometric functions \cos\theta_r and \sin\theta_r are transcendental and cannot be handled natively by QE backends.

B.2 Algebraic Encoding

We introduce ghost variables c = \cos\theta_r and s = \sin\theta_r governed by polynomial ODEs:

\dot{c} = -s \cdot \omega, \quad \dot{s} = c \cdot \omega, \quad \dot{\theta}_r = \omega

with the algebraic invariant:

c^2 + s^2 = 1

Lemma B.1. The unit circle invariant c^2 + s^2 = 1 is a differential invariant.

Proof. Compute:

\frac{d}{dt}(c^2 + s^2) = 2c\dot{c} + 2s\dot{s} = 2c(-s\omega) + 2s(c\omega) = 0

Thus c^2 + s^2 is constant and equals 1 if initially so. \square

B.3 KeYmaera X Model

```
ProgramVariables
  Real rx, ry;      /* Position */
  Real vx, vy;      /* Velocity components */
  Real c, s;        /* Cosine and Sine ghost variables */
  Real omega;       /* Angular steering rate */
  Real a;           /* Longitudinal acceleration */
End

Problem
  /* Initial state includes trigonometric unit circle invariant */
  (c^2 + s^2 = 1 & vr >= 0) ->
  [
    {
      { omega := *; ?(omega >= -omega_max & omega <= omega_max); }
      {
        rx' = vr * c, ry' = vr * s, vr' = a,
        c' = -s * omega, s' = c * omega
        & c^2 + s^2 = 1
      }
    }*
  ]
  (c^2 + s^2 = 1)
End
```

B.4 Polynomial Structure Preservation

Theorem B.1. The ghost-variable encoding preserves polynomial structure of the hybrid program, enabling quantifier elimination.

Proof. The dynamics \dot{c} = -s\omega and \dot{s} = c\omega are polynomial in (c, s, \omega). The invariant c^2 + s^2 = 1 is polynomial. The safety condition is expressed entirely in terms of (p_{rx}, p_{ry}, v_r, c, s, p_{ox}, p_{oy}, v_{ox}, v_{oy}), all of which evolve according to polynomial differential equations. Therefore, QE applies. \square

