Appendix C: Friction Circle Model and Coupled Braking-Deceleration Limit

C.1 Physical Derivation

Definition C.1 (Friction Circle). Under Coulomb friction, the vector sum of longitudinal and lateral forces cannot exceed the maximum available friction:

\sqrt{F_{\text{long}}^2 + F_{\text{lat}}^2} \leq F_{\max} = \mu m g

where F_{\text{long}} = m a and F_{\text{lat}} = m v_r \omega. Equation [2.40] in the source literature expresses this as:

\left(\frac{F_x}{F_z}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{F_y}{F_z}\right)^2 \leq \mu^2

which plots as a circle called the "Friction Circle".

C.2 Coupled Deceleration Limit

Theorem C.1 (Coupled Braking-Deceleration Limit). The maximum longitudinal braking deceleration under combined slip is:

a_{\max}(v_r, \omega) = \sqrt{(\mu g)^2 - (v_r \omega)^2}

Proof. Substituting the force expressions into the friction circle constraint:

\sqrt{(ma)^2 + (m v_r \omega)^2} \leq \mu m g

Dividing by m and squaring:

a^2 + (v_r \omega)^2 \leq (\mu g)^2

Isolating a^2:

a^2 \leq (\mu g)^2 - (v_r \omega)^2

Taking the square root:

a \leq \sqrt{(\mu g)^2 - (v_r \omega)^2}

The maximum braking deceleration is therefore a_{\max}(v_r, \omega) = \sqrt{(\mu g)^2 - (v_r \omega)^2}. \square

Corollary C.1 (Physical Interpretation).

· Zero turn (\omega = 0): a_{\max} = \mu g (full braking available).
· Pure spin (v_r \omega = \mu g): a_{\max} = 0 (no braking available).
· If v_r \omega > \mu g: slip is mathematically guaranteed.

C.3 Dynamic Passive Safety

The constant deceleration parameter b is replaced by the state-dependent coupled limit:

\phi_{\text{ps, dynamic}} \equiv (v_r = 0) \lor \left( \|\mathbf{p}_r - \mathbf{p}_o\| > \frac{v_r^2}{2 a_{\max}(v_r, \omega)} + V \frac{v_r}{a_{\max}(v_r, \omega)} \right)

C.4 Burckhardt Tire Model Parameters

The Burckhardt/Kiencke model characterizes friction between tires and ground as a function of slip ratio \sigma and three parameters specific to the ground surface:

\mu(\sigma) = c_1 \left( 1 - e^{-c_2 \sigma} \right) - c_3 \sigma

For dry concrete surface: c_1 = 1.197, c_2 = 25.17, c_3 = 0.54.

