Appendix D: Real-Time Friction Estimation

D.1 Recursive Least Squares Formulation

Definition D.1 (Regressor Formulation). The friction estimation problem is formulated as a linear regression:

y(t) = \phi(t)^T \theta + \epsilon(t)

where y(t) = a_{\text{imu}}(t) is the measured acceleration, \phi(t) = g is the regressor, \theta = \mu is the parameter to estimate, and \epsilon(t) is noise.

Definition D.2 (RLS Update). The Recursive Least Squares update with forgetting factor \lambda \in (0.95, 0.99):

\hat{\theta}(t) = \hat{\theta}(t-1) + K(t) \left( y(t) - \phi(t)^T \hat{\theta}(t-1) \right)

K(t) = \frac{P(t-1)\phi(t)}{\lambda + \phi(t)^T P(t-1) \phi(t)}

P(t) = \frac{1}{\lambda} \left( P(t-1) - \frac{P(t-1)\phi(t)\phi(t)^T P(t-1)}{\lambda + \phi(t)^T P(t-1)\phi(t)} \right)

Due to linear parametrization, it is possible to estimate unknown and assumed slowly changing coefficients by RLS methods with a forgetting scheme discounting past data. RLS is widely applied in mobile robotics, including skid-steering robots for friction compensation and localization.

D.2 Active Probing

Definition D.3 (Micro-Pulse Injection). During constant-velocity cruising, inject a high-frequency micro-torque pulse \Delta \tau of duration < 20 ms into the drive motors.

Definition D.4 (Contact Stiffness). The contact stiffness is estimated as:

k = \frac{\Delta a}{\Delta \tau}

where \Delta a is the measured acceleration response.

D.3 Conservative Bounding

Definition D.5 (Safe Friction Bound). The safety monitor uses a conservative statistical lower bound:

\mu_{\text{safe}}(t) = \hat{\mu}(t) - k \cdot \sigma_{\mu}(t)

where \sigma_{\mu}(t) is the estimation variance and k \in [2.5, 3.0] is a safety factor corresponding to a 99.7% confidence interval.

Theorem D.1 (Safety Guarantee Under Friction Uncertainty). If the true friction \mu_{\text{true}} satisfies \mu_{\text{true}} \geq \mu_{\text{safe}}, then the dynamic passive safety condition \phi_{\text{ps, dynamic}} with \mu = \mu_{\text{safe}} is conservative.

Proof. Since a_{\max}(v_r, \omega; \mu) = \sqrt{(\mu g)^2 - (v_r \omega)^2} is monotonically increasing in \mu, using \mu_{\text{safe}} \leq \mu_{\text{true}} yields a_{\max}(\mu_{\text{safe}}) \leq a_{\max}(\mu_{\text{true}}), hence the required separation is larger, and the condition is more conservative. \square

