Appendix E: Neural Barrier Certificate Verification

E.1 ReLU Network Formulation

Definition E.1 (ReLU Network). A ReLU network is a function f: \mathbb{R}^n \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^m of the form:

f(x) = W_L \sigma(W_{L-1} \sigma(\cdots \sigma(W_1 x + b_1) \cdots) + b_{L-1}) + b_L

where \sigma(z) = \max(0, z) is the ReLU activation.

E.2 Barrier Certificate Conditions

Definition E.2 (Neural Barrier Certificate). A neural barrier certificate B_{\text{NN}}: \mathbb{R}^n \rightarrow \mathbb{R} is a ReLU neural network satisfying:

1. B_{\text{NN}}(\xi) \leq 0 for all \xi \in \mathcal{X}_0 (initial set),
2. B_{\text{NN}}(\xi) > 0 for all \xi \in \mathcal{X}_u (unsafe set),
3. \dot{B}_{\text{NN}}(\xi) \leq 0 for all \xi on the boundary B_{\text{NN}}(\xi) = 0.

E.3 Verification and Falsification

A necessary and sufficient condition for verifying the correctness of ReLU neural barrier certificates has been established. For ReLU networks, the partitioning of the input space exhibits a special structure: every n-dimensional linear region shares a facet with each of its adjacent linear regions.

Algorithm E.1 (Boundary Propagation).

1. Initialize the set of valid activation indicators as \mathcal{A} := \{\mathscr{C}_0\} and the visited set as \mathcal{B} := \emptyset.
2. Select an activation indicator \mathscr{C} \in \mathcal{A} \setminus \mathcal{B} and add \mathscr{C} to \mathcal{B}.
3. For each facet j of \mathcal{X}(\mathscr{C}), solve the LP feasibility problem to find intersection with the boundary.
4. If the LP admits a feasible solution, enumerate all feasible activation indicators activated by the solution.
5. Terminate when \mathcal{A} = \mathcal{B}, meaning all valid activation indicators have been explored.

E.4 Branch-and-Bound Verification

For scalability, bound propagation techniques and the Branch-and-Bound scheme efficiently verify that a neural network satisfies the conditions to be a control barrier function over the continuous state space. The approach leverages the piecewise linearity of ReLU networks to enumerate valid linear regions and verify or falsify the three barrier certificate conditions in each region.

Theorem E.1 (Soundness). If the barrier certificate produced by Algorithm E.1 passes verification, then it is a valid barrier certificate.

Proof. The algorithm enumerates all valid linear regions of the ReLU network. By verifying the three barrier conditions in each region, and because the union of all valid linear regions covers the boundary \partial\mathcal{C}, the certificate satisfies all conditions globally. \square

