Appendix F: Matrix Zonotope Data-Driven Verification

F.1 Data-Consistent Model Set

Definition F.1 (Matrix Zonotope). A matrix zonotope is a set:

\mathcal{MZ} = \left\{ M_0 + \sum_{i=1}^{N} \alpha_i M_i : \alpha_i \in [-1, 1] \right\}

where M_0 is the center matrix and M_i are the generator matrices.

Definition F.2 (Data-Consistent Model Set). Given noisy observations \{(\xi_k, \xi_{k+1})\}_{k=1}^{N}, the data-consistent model set is:

\Sigma = \left\{ (A, B) : \xi_{k+1} = A \xi_k + B u_k + w_k, \ \|w_k\| \leq \epsilon \right\}

F.2 Data-Driven Barrier Certificate

A data-driven safety verification framework leverages matrix zonotopes and barrier certificates to verify system safety directly from noisy data. Instead of trusting a single unreliable model, the framework constructs a set of models that capture all possible system dynamics consistent with the observed data, ensuring that the true system model is always contained within this set. This model set is compactly represented using matrix zonotopes, enabling efficient computation and propagation of uncertainty.

Theorem F.1 (Data-Driven Barrier Certificate). If there exists a barrier certificate B satisfying the three conditions for all models in the data-consistent model set \Sigma, then B is a valid barrier certificate for the true system.

Proof. By construction, the true system model is contained in \Sigma. If the barrier conditions hold for all models in \Sigma, they hold in particular for the true model. \square

F.3 Three-Layer Certificate Architecture

Architecture F.1 (Three-Layer Certificate System).

1. Formal core: KeYmaera X proof of Theorem 3.2 for the idealized braking model, providing the normative proof certificate.
2. Runtime certificate: Neural barrier certificate B_{\text{NN}} trained on hardware data (braking behavior, actuator delays, tire slip, surface variations). The runtime monitor verifies B_{\text{NN}}(\xi) \leq 0.
3. Conservative fallback: If B_{\text{NN}} cannot be evaluated due to sensor fault or numerical uncertainty, revert to B_{\text{ana}}(\xi) \leq 0.

Theorem F.2 (Soundness of Three-Layer Architecture). The three-layer architecture is sound: if any layer certifies safety, the system is safe.

Proof. The formal core certifies safety by Theorem 3.2. The runtime certificate certifies safety by Theorem E.1 (if verified offline). The conservative fallback certifies safety by Theorem 4.1. Since all three layers certify the same safety property, satisfaction of any one is sufficient. \square

