H.1 Operating Area Classifications

ISO 3691-4:2024 specifies safety requirements for driverless industrial trucks and their systems. The condition of the operating zone has a significant effect on the safe operation of the driverless industrial truck. Preparations of the operating zone to eliminate associated hazards are specified in Annex A.

Table H.1: Operating Area Definitions and PULSE-SAFE Response

Operating Area Definition Minimum Clearance PULSE-SAFE Response
Operating area ≥ 0.5 m lateral clearance, ≥ 2.1 m height 0.5 m Full speed (≤ 1.0 m/s)
Operating hazard area Insufficient clearance or unprotected Marked Reduced speed (≤ 0.5 m/s)
Restricted area Personnel detection cannot protect Marked Safe stop on entry
Closed area No personnel access None Full speed (≤ 2.0 m/s)

In the operating hazard area, protective measures include: providing escape passage (0.5 m wide, 2.1 m high), limiting maximum speed, audible/visual warnings, and delayed muting (when the distance between AGV and load is sufficiently small, e.g., < 180 mm, ensuring personnel cannot enter before activating the muting function).

H.2 Speed Limits

Definition H.1 (Operating Area State). PULSE-SAFE maintains a runtime operating area state \alpha \in \{\text{op, hazard, restricted, closed}\} derived from map data and sensor fusion. The speed limit is:

v_{\max}(\alpha) =
\begin{cases}
2.0 \text{ m/s}, & \alpha = \text{closed} \\
1.0 \text{ m/s}, & \alpha = \text{op} \\
0.5 \text{ m/s}, & \alpha = \text{hazard} \\
0 \text{ m/s}, & \alpha = \text{restricted}
\end{cases}

If operating speed > 1.2 m/s, the AGV operating area shall be treated as a closed area, applying closed-area safety requirements. The displacement speed equal to or lower than 300 mm/s is established by EN ISO 3691-4 to be able to move without means of detection of persons.

H.3 Incomplete Machine Problem

AGVs are typically supplied as incomplete machines. The complete system must be regarded as an "assembly of machinery," requiring a certificate of conformity. The party responsible (manufacturer or system integrator) must be determined early in the project.

